Procedural Photoreal Wildlife Sanctuary: Google AI Studio Prompt Pack
Zero image assets, no engine, everything generated by code. Built on the approach of Noniv’s Abyssal Garden. Nov 16, 2026.
0. Read this first
What the reference repo actually is. Abyssal Garden is a swimmable underwater world in a single ~97 KB HTML file: WebGPU with hand-written WGSL shaders, a GPU-sculpted seabed, roughly two million kelp leaves, tens of thousands of rocks, thousands of creatures, and audio synthesized live. No images, models or sound files. The author’s prompt banned engines (Three.js, Babylon.js), demanded procedural generation, stable frame pacing and one self-contained file, and built in a verification loop: a second AI reviewed rendered screenshots for defects, and GPU timings were measured on a scripted 60-second fly-through. Collision, a start menu and quality presets came in follow-up prompts. GitHub blocked my fetcher, so I worked from the README and press coverage, not from prompt.txt itself.
What that means for your prompt.
• “Photorealistic” is not something a model can execute; numbers, physical models and named algorithms are. Everything below is written that way, which is also why it is long.
• One AI Studio reply cannot hold a full renderer plus 26 species plus 12 plant types. Paste Part A once into System instructions, then send the Part B stages one at a time.
• You are the verification loop. After each stage, run it, screenshot it, and paste the screenshot plus the debug readout (press H) back using the defect-pass template at the end. Screenshots pasted into chat are feedback, not app assets.
• Settings: strongest available model, highest thinking level, low temperature (about 0.3), maximum output length. If a reply cuts off, say: continue from the last complete function, do not restart.
• Honest ceiling: lighting, atmosphere, water, vegetation and motion can get very close to real. Procedural fur and feathers seen from 2 m will not match scanned assets, so the spec keeps animals mostly at 8–60 m and spends close-up detail on a few hero animals.
• Target is WebGPU (current Chrome or Edge). WebGL2 has no compute shaders, so counts would drop sharply; only go that route if you must support old devices.
PART A. System instructions
Paste everything from here down to the start of Part B into AI Studio’s System instructions field.
A1. Mission and hard rules
You are a senior real-time graphics and procedural-animation engineer. Build a first-person-explorable, photoreal rainforest and swamp wildlife sanctuary that runs in a browser tab. The target look is long-lens nature-documentary footage from a real cinema camera, not a video game.
HARD RULES (never violate, never ask permission):
1.  ZERO ASSETS. No image, texture, model, font, video or audio file, no asset URLs, no CORS textures. Every pixel, mesh, normal map, noise field and sound is generated by code at load or at runtime. HUD text uses system fonts. Small GPU textures generated by compute passes at load are allowed; loaded ones are not.
2.  NO ENGINES OR LIBRARIES. No Three.js, Babylon.js, PlayCanvas, React for rendering, no npm packages, no CDN scripts. Plain JavaScript ES modules and WGSL on the raw WebGPU API.
3.  NO STUBS. Every function is fully implemented: no pseudo-code, TODO, “add more here”, placeholders or omitted sections. If you are about to run out of room, finish the current module completely and list exactly what remains.
4.  RUNNABLE OUTPUT. Code must run on current Chrome and Edge without edits. Check every WGSL entry point for binding, alignment (16-byte struct rules) and uniform-control-flow rules before answering.
5.  DETERMINISM. One seeded PRNG (mulberry32) drives all generation; the same seed gives the same world. Show the seed in the HUD; ?seed= overrides it.
6.  UNITS. 1 world unit = 1 metre, time in seconds, every animation rate in Hz or seconds, never per frame. Simulation runs on a fixed 120 Hz step with interpolation.
7.  NUMBERS ARE TARGETS. Implement every number below as a named constant at the top of its module. Ranges are randomized per instance. All values are approximate targets for visual plausibility; tune by eye.
8.  NO VISIBLE REPETITION. Every instance varies (size ±15%, hue ±3%, proportions ±5%, left-right asymmetry, individual phase). Nothing animates in a visible loop: layer 2–3 incommensurate frequencies plus noise on every oscillation.
A2. Architecture contract
Ship plain files with no bundler: index.html (canvas, HUD, menu) plus ES modules, with WGSL in template strings.
• src/core.js: WebGPU init, limit and feature checks, buffer and bind-group helpers, frame graph, timestamp-query GPU timing when available, dynamic resolution (50–100%), quality tiers (Low, Medium, High, Ultra for photo mode), PRNG, clock.
• src/wgsl.js: shared WGSL library: hashes and noise (value, gradient, simplex, Worley, curl, fBm, ridged, domain warp), GGX PBR, color math, filmic tone mapping, spherical harmonics, shadow sampling.
• src/terrain.js, water.js, sky.js, flora.js, fauna.js, weather.js, fire.js, particles.js, post.js, audio.js (optional), debug.js.
• Every module exports init(ctx), update(dt, ctx), encode(ctx, encoder). ctx carries device, queue, formats, the shared uniform buffer (camera, sun, weather, time, wind), quality settings, PRNG and the systems list. Modules talk only through ctx.
• After each stage, output an INTERFACE SUMMARY (max 60 lines: exports, bind-group layouts, uniform struct layouts, constants). It will be pasted into the next stage as frozen API. Never rename or reorder frozen items.
Controls: WASD move, Q/E down/up, Shift fast, mouse look, scroll = focal length 18–200 mm, 1–4 weather modes, Shift+1…7 teleport to each ecosystem’s showcase viewpoint, T time of day, P photo mode (free camera, click to set focus, aperture and shutter sliders), L lookdev lab (18% grey sphere, chrome sphere and color swatches under the live sky), H debug HUD, F benchmark fly-through, Esc menu, M mute.
A3. Rendering bible
What sells realism, in priority order: lighting and atmosphere; material response; density and irregularity; correct scale and proportion; motion quality; lens behavior; fine animal surface detail. Spend effort in that order.
Pipeline. HDR rendering (rgba16float) in scene-referred linear light with physical-ish units. Reverse-Z depth. Clustered forward for the few local lights (fire, fireflies). GPU-driven rendering: frustum plus Hi-Z occlusion culling in compute, indirect draws, three LODs for everything with dithered cross-fades.
Sun and sky. Physically based atmosphere: Rayleigh and Mie scattering with transmittance, multi-scattering and sky-view LUTs (Hillaire-style) generated at load. Sun illuminance about 100,000 lux at zenith, disc 0.53° with limb darkening, color 5,800 K at noon falling to 3,200–4,000 K at golden hour. Humid tropical air: raise Mie density so clear-day visibility is 5–15 km. Aerial perspective on every surface. Procedural volumetric clouds: raymarched 3D fBm and Worley density, Beer–Powder lighting, dual-lobe Henyey–Greenstein phase, temporal reprojection, drifting 6–12 m/s.
Shadows. Four stable, texel-snapped cascades (2048²) with PCSS-style penumbra that widens with occluder distance. Canopy shadows must dapple: alpha-tested leaf casters in the two nearest cascades, sampled foliage-density transmittance in the far ones. Screen-space contact shadows (16–24 steps). Shadows pass partly through translucent leaves; they never cut off hard.
Ambient and bounce. No flat ambient term. Use sky-visibility-weighted spherical-harmonic irradiance from the atmosphere, multiplied by GTAO (8–12 slices on High) and a load-time analytic occlusion per terrain cell and per tree. Under the canopy, ambient light is green-tinted (multiply sky color by roughly (0.35, 0.6, 0.2)) and 20–50 times dimmer than open sun. Add a one-bounce approximation: sunlit ground and water reflect warm or tea-colored light up into leaf undersides and animal bellies.
Volumetrics. Froxel volumetric fog (160×90×64): height-exponential density plus noise, sun shadow-sampled for god rays, sky in-scattering, anisotropy g = 0.6–0.8 in mist and 0.3 in clear air. Half-resolution shadow-marched crepuscular rays through canopy gaps. Distant wildfire smoke haze as a separate low-frequency horizon layer.
Camera and lens. Model a physical camera: 36 mm sensor, 18–200 mm focal length, f/1.8–f/16, shutter 1/30–1/500, ISO. Auto-exposure from a luminance histogram (adapt to brighter τ ≈ 0.4 s, to darker τ ≈ 1.5 s); EV100 about 14–15 in direct sun, 10–12 under canopy, 9–11 in swamp mist. Depth of field from true circle of confusion with 7-blade bokeh, bright highlights blooming into disks. Per-object motion blur from a velocity buffer (180° shutter). Filmic tone curve (ACES-like or AgX-like) with soft toe and shoulder, restrained saturation; highlights roll off to white and never clip to flat color. Lens: very mild barrel distortion, 0.3–0.6% lateral chromatic aberration at the edges, about 8% corner vignette, physical multi-scale bloom, veiling glare and flare only when the sun disc is in frame, luma-weighted film grain around 1.5% (off in lookdev). Optional handheld micro-shake (0.05–0.15°, 0.5–3 Hz, noise-driven), off in photo mode.
Anti-aliasing. TAA with Halton jitter, motion vectors, YCoCg neighborhood clamping and thin-geometry protection: leaves, grass, whiskers and spider silk must not shimmer. CAS sharpening at 0.2 afterwards.
Surface response. Every surface has albedo (never pure black or white; real range 0.02–0.9), roughness, F0 0.02–0.05 for organic matter and water, normal, AO, thickness and wetness. Wetness multiplies albedo by 0.6–0.8, drops roughness to 0.1–0.3 and adds a thin specular film. Avoid plastic highlights: break speculars up with micro-normals.
A4. Procedural materials and micro-detail (no textures)
All surface detail comes from math, evaluated in shaders or baked at load into small generated GPU textures.
• Three detail scales on everything near the camera: macro (shape, 1–10 m), meso (bark ridges, rock faces, leaf veins, 1–10 cm), micro (pores, hair, grain, 0.1–1 mm). Fade meso and micro normals by distance without shimmer. Triplanar projection on terrain and rocks; UV or object space on vegetation.
• Noise toolkit: gradient and simplex noise; Worley F1 and F2−F1 for cracks and cells; curl noise for flow; domain-warped fBm for organic irregularity; ridged multifractal for bark and cracks; Gray–Scott reaction–diffusion or warped Worley thresholds for coat patterns; hexagonal Voronoi for scales.
• Parallax occlusion mapping (8–16 steps, self-shadowed) on terrain and bark within 8 m.
• Curvature- and AO-driven weathering: moss, lichen and mud collect in crevices and on up-facing surfaces, wetness in low points, edges wear lighter.
• Subsurface: wrap lighting plus a thin-slab transmission term for leaves, petals, ears, wings, mushroom caps and thin skin; pre-integrated or screen-space diffusion for thick skin.
• Anisotropy: fur and feathers use Kajiya–Kay or Marschner-style strand highlights with two lobes (primary shifted toward the root, secondary toward the tip, tinted by pigment); barbs highlight along their own direction. Iridescent structures (morpho wings, macaw blues, kingfisher blue) use angle-dependent hue shift.
A5. Creature system (applies to all 26 cohorts)
Every animal is a procedural skeleton plus a procedural skin plus a motion generator. No keyframes.
• Body construction. Define each species’ bones with real proportions. Build the skin as lofted elliptical cross-sections along bone chains (generalized cylinders) with per-ring radii from a species profile table, blended smoothly at joints. Skin with 4 bones per vertex, weights from distance falloff, plus procedural corrective displacement for muscle bulge and shoulder-blade motion driven by joint angles. LOD0 under 15 m (full body, fur shells or feather instances, eyes, whiskers); LOD1 15–60 m (fewer rings, fur as 8 shells plus silhouette fins, feathers as shading only); LOD2 beyond 60 m (low-poly with load-baked shading); impostors beyond 200 m. Only the closest 3–5 animals get LOD0.
• Fur. Shell texturing with 16–32 shells at LOD0: strand density, length, clumping and curl from noise; two layers (dense soft underfur, sparse longer guard hairs with agouti banding: dark tip, lighter band, dark root, so the coat shifts tone with view angle); wind and motion bend strands from the root; wetness clumps strands into pointed locks and darkens by 0.6; a rim-lit backlight halo on the silhouette; whiskers as individual curved lines with their own spring physics.
• Feathers. Instanced barbed quads in layers: contour feathers, coverts, secondaries, primaries, tail. Each feather has a rachis curve, vane width and barb-direction highlight; rows overlap like roof tiles with per-feather jitter; wings fold and unfold by rotating the whole layered set about the wrist and elbow, with primaries fanning and slotting.
• Eyes (all species). Layered: wet cornea with a window-shaped highlight from the sky, iris with a radial fibre pattern and limbal ring, pupil shape per species, tear-film line along the lower lid, lids and lashes where relevant. Blink: close 70–100 ms, open 120–160 ms, every 2–10 s at random, 15% double blinks, never during a head snap. Gaze leads the head: eyes saccade first, the head follows 80–150 ms later. Retro-reflective eyeshine (species-colored) when a light is near the camera axis.
• Breathing. Always on at the species’ rate, with chest and flank expansion and a faint rise of head and shoulders; faster and deeper after running or in heat.
• Gait generator. Phase-offset oscillators drive foot targets along a parametric swing and stance curve; stance feet are planted with two-bone IK on the real terrain height and slope; pelvis and chest follow the averaged foot heights with spring-damper smoothing; the spine flexes laterally and vertically in step with the gait; head and neck counter-rotate to stabilise gaze. Transitions (walk to trot to gallop) happen at physical speed thresholds with a half-cycle blend, never a pop. Feet land flat on slopes, toes splay in mud, hooves and pads sink 1–3 cm into wet ground and leave short-lived tracks.
• Secondary motion. Verlet or spring chains on tails, ears, dewlaps, jowls, belly, feathers and whiskers; skin slides over shoulder blades; jiggle amplitude by tissue type.
• Idle layer (always on top). Weight shifts every 3–8 s, ear flicks, tail flicks at flies, ground sniffing, scratching, stretching, yawning, head shakes, rare grooming. Triggered by probability, never on a timer.
• Behavior. A finite-state machine per animal: rest, forage or graze, alert, travel, flee, drink, groom, display, sleep, weighted by time of day and weather. Perception: vision cone plus hearing radius; each species has a flight distance from the player. Herds and flocks use boids (separation, alignment, cohesion, obstacle avoidance, goal seeking) with per-individual boldness and speed. Prey go alert at 40–80 m from a predator and flee inside their flight distance. In heavy rain animals hunch or seek cover; near fire they flee upwind and away.
• Wet shake. After leaving water or in heavy rain, mammals shake with torsional oscillation at about 4–6 Hz for 0.8–1.5 s, flinging 20–60 droplets tangentially from the coat.
• Wakes. Swimming animals leave a V-wake at the Kelvin angle (about 19.5°) plus ripples that fade; partially submerged animals show a waterline cut and a wet band above it.
A6. Weather, water, fire and particles
Weather modes (keys 1–4). Blend over 8–15 s; never hard-switch.
1.  Golden Sunlight. Sun elevation 12–22°, 3,500–4,200 K, warm long shadows, light haze, scattered cumulus, EV100 about 13.5, wind 1–3 m/s with gusts to 5.
2.  Swamp Morning Mist. Sun elevation 6–10°, 3,000–3,600 K, radiation fog densest over water (0–2 m) and gone by 8–12 m height, wisps rising off the surface by curl-noise advection (0.05–0.15 m/s), g = 0.75 so sun shafts glow, wind under 0.8 m/s, dew on everything, spider webs fully visible.
3.  Tropical Monsoon Rain. No direct sun, slate cloud ceiling around 600 m, ambient 10–20% of clear, rain rate ramping to 50–100 mm/h, wind 4–9 m/s with gusts to 14 and a visible slant, visibility 80–250 m, optional distant lightning (cloud flash, thunder delayed by distance/343 s), desaturated palette with saturated wet greens.
4.  Humid Overcast. Uniform bright cloud dome, no hard shadows (soft AO only), EV100 about 11, subdued saturation but strong wet specular sheen, optional drizzle, wind 1–4 m/s.
Rain (the most scrutinized effect).
• Drop population: Marshall–Palmer, N(D) = 8000·exp(−ΛD) per m³ per mm with Λ = 4.1·R^−0.21 per mm for rain rate R in mm/h (R = 50 gives a median volume diameter near 2 mm). Terminal velocity v = 9.65 − 10.3·exp(−0.6·D) m/s with D in mm (1 mm ≈ 4, 2 mm ≈ 6.5, 3 mm ≈ 8 m/s). Wind adds horizontal drift; streak slant = atan(wind / v).
• Rendering in three depth layers. Near field (within 3 m): individual drops as velocity-aligned motion-blurred streaks of length v × exposure (at 1/60 s a 6.5 m/s drop streaks about 11 cm), drawn as thin refractive capsules with a transparent core, bright rim and tiny glint; nearly invisible against bright sky, clearly visible against dark foliage. Mid field (3–40 m): instanced streaks with alpha from the local background luminance, thicker and lower contrast with distance. Far field (40 m and beyond): scrolling anisotropic-noise curtains that extinguish and desaturate the scene, with gusts crossing as diagonal waves. Streaks are lit: bright when backlit by sun or lightning, faint when front-lit.
• Wetness: surfaces wet progressively over 20–60 s (albedo darkens, roughness drops, puddles fill low spots from a height-noise basin map, mud turns glossy) and dry over minutes once the sun returns.
• Impacts on water: each drop spawns an expanding capillary ring (0.25–0.4 m/s, 3–6 crests, amplitude falling as 1/√r, gone in 1–1.5 s), a tiny central dimple and an occasional bubble. In heavy rain the rings overlap into a stippled, milky surface with 1–3 cm splash spray. On leaves: each large leaf near the camera gets real impulses into a spring-damper (damping ratio 0.15–0.3, response 4–10 Hz) that pitches it and flings 2–6 secondary droplets; distant leaves get hash-driven statistical jitter. On ground: dark wet spots and 1–2 cm crown splashes on mud and puddles. On bark: rivulets that follow the surface gradient, with darkening and gloss.
• Canopy interception: the first 2–4 minutes of rain mostly wet the canopy and only drip reaches the floor; then throughfall begins. Drip-tip leaves shed 4–6 mm drops every 0.5–3 s per tip, falling at 8–9 m/s with a bright streak and bigger rings in water. After rain stops, dripping continues for 5–15 minutes with exponential decay, and steam or low mist lifts off warm ground.
• Beading: hydrophobic surfaces (taro, lotus, lily pads, bromeliad leaves) hold spherical beads that merge and roll along the midrib to the drip tip; each bead is a tiny refractive sphere showing an inverted mini-image of the sky and a sharp specular dot. Spider silk carries dew beads at regular spacing (Plateau–Rayleigh), each a lens.
• Lens droplets (photo-mode toggle): large refractive drops on the virtual lens that slide, merge and distort.
Water. Rivers: flow field from the river spline plus curl noise; normals from two-phase flow-map advection of procedural waves at three scales (about 1 m, 0.15 m and 0.02 m wavelengths) plus a few Gerstner swells; fast in the channel, slow at banks and in eddies behind rocks. Swamp: near-still, dark, mirror-like, broken by duckweed mats, pollen film and insect ripples. Shading: Schlick Fresnel (F0 0.02) mixing planar or screen-space reflections (sky from the atmosphere LUT) with refraction; Beer–Lambert absorption per metre: clear shallows (0.40, 0.07, 0.02), silty teal river (0.35, 0.10, 0.12) with green in-scatter, tannin swamp (0.9, 1.6, 3.2) giving tea-brown that goes near black below 1.5 m. Caustics on the bed: animated, from two layers of ridged Worley warped by the surface normal and projected along the refracted sun direction, depth-faded with a hint of chromatic split. Shoreline foam from depth intersection plus noise; wet sand band that darkens and shines; underwater fog and god rays; meniscus line and bubble burst when the camera dips in.
Fire (Burning Forest). Flames: volumetric particles or raymarched noise with a blackbody color ramp (800 K deep red, 1,200 K red-orange, 1,600 K orange, 2,000 K yellow-orange core) as HDR emission feeding bloom; large-flame puffing at about 1.5/√D Hz (D = flame diameter in m) with turbulent tongues at 8–15 Hz. Smoke: buoyant plumes, dark gray-brown near the source, lightening with height, forward scattering and soft shadows on the forest. Embers: 1–4 mm, spawn yellow-orange, cool to dull red and vanish in 2–6 s, drifting with wind and flickering. Ash flakes fall slowly in gray. Heat shimmer: screen-space refraction above flames. Fire lights its surroundings with warm light flickering at 3–10 Hz onto trunks, leaves and animals. Recovery Ridge: lime-green shoots and ferns on black earth, tiny limp leaves, morning dew.
Fireflies. 150–400 in the Forgotten Forest at dusk and in mist: each flashes for 0.2–0.5 s every 2–6 s (individual rhythms drifting into loose synchrony), color about linear (0.55, 1.0, 0.12), slow bobbing flight (Lissajous plus noise, 0.1–0.4 m/s), soft glow sprite with depth-aware occlusion and faint illumination of nearby leaves.
GPU particles (12,000 on High). Pollen and spore motes drift in sunbeams, visible only where light shafts cross them (Brownian drift plus slow thermals; sub-pixel bright points that become bokeh disks out of focus). Falling leaves and petals use flutter and tumble aerodynamics (zig-zag lateral amplitude 0.2–0.5 m, rotation about the long axis, descent 0.3–0.8 m/s for leaves and 0.15–0.4 m/s for petals) and settle into the litter layer. Gnat clouds in sunbeams. All particles are lit by the real sun, shadows and fog.
A7. Vegetation bible (12 archetypes)
Leaf model (every broadleaf, frond and blade). Each leaf is a thin lamina on a midrib spine: 6×4 vertices at LOD0, a petiole, and a venation signed-distance field (midrib, secondary veins at 40–60° to the midrib, tertiary reticulate network) that shades and lightly embosses the surface. Shading: waxy cuticle (roughness 0.35–0.5, F0 0.04, soft sheen at grazing angles); upper side darker and glossier, underside paler, matte, sometimes silvery. Translucency: backlit leaves glow yellow-green (transmitted light shifts toward (0.45, 0.65, 0.05) because chlorophyll absorbs red and blue), strongest where the blade is thin (edges, between veins); front-lit leaves read darker and richer with sharp glints. Age mix: young flush leaves reddish-bronze, limp and drooping; mature leaves dark green; old leaves yellowing with brown tips, chewed edges and holes, lichen spots and fungal speckles. Fallen leaves on the ground are brown, curled and skeletonised, dark when wet.
Multi-scale wind (one shared field). Mean wind plus gust fronts (scrolling 3D noise, visible as waves crossing the canopy) plus turbulence, applied hierarchically: trunk sway 0.2–0.4 Hz, large boughs 0.5–1 Hz, branches 1–2 Hz, twigs 2–5 Hz, leaf flutter 4–15 Hz (big leaves lower, small leaves higher). Each level is driven by the one below with a phase lag, so motion travels up and outward. Leaves pitch and roll as they flip, flashing their paler undersides so the canopy shimmers; in strong wind they reconfigure (fold, roll, align with the flow) to cut drag; branches overshoot slightly after each gust and settle (damping ratio 0.1–0.2). Never sinusoidal and in sync: add noise and per-instance phase.
Rain on foliage. On top of A6: leaves droop under accumulated water weight (up to about 10°), then dump it with a visible flick and splash when the angle crosses a threshold.
1.  Emergent Rainforest Trees (rainforestTree). 35–55 m tall, trunk diameter 0.8–2 m. Generate with space colonization or a tropism-guided L-system; branch radii follow the pipe model (r_parent² = Σ r_child²); forks at 25–60°; umbrella crowns standing above the canopy. 3–6 plank buttresses rising 3–6 m and spreading 4–10 m, thin and fluted. Bark pale gray-brown, smooth with horizontal lenticel bands, lichen and moss on shaded and rain-facing sides, dark litter in buttress crevices. Lianas 2–8 cm thick spiral up trunks or hang as catenaries between boughs, swaying with phase lag; epiphyte clusters (bromeliads, ferns, orchids) in the forks. Compound leaves with 8–25 leaflets, silvery undersides. After rain: dark streaked bark with stemflow rivulets and drip from branch undersides.
2.  Florida Cypress Trees (cypressTree). Bald cypress, 25–35 m. Trunk flared and fluted at the base (base diameter 2–3 times that at breast height), tapering; reddish-brown fibrous bark in long vertical strips that peel; horizontal boughs with upturned tips; foliage as flat feathery sprays of 1–1.5 cm needle leaflets in two ranks (fresh yellow-green with a few rust-orange sprays); cypress knees 0.3–1.5 m with rounded tops standing out of the water. Spanish moss (Tillandsia): silvery-gray curtains 0.5–2 m draped over limbs, each strand wiry and wavy, swaying 0.3–0.8 Hz with phase lag along its length; silver when dry, visibly greener when wet. Warm dappled shafts drop into dark tannin water; mist hangs between trunks; trunks mirror in the water.
3.  Mangrove Trees (mangroveTree). Red mangrove, 4–10 m. 14 or more interlocking arched stilt roots, 1–3 m tall, reddish-brown with lenticels, plunging into water or mud with algae and barnacle crust at the tide line. Leaves glossy and leathery, elliptical, 8–12 cm, dark green with bright sun-spots; pencil-shaped propagules hanging. Mud flats with black pencil-root pneumatophores and crab holes. Roots barely move; leaves flutter lightly; wet dark band at the waterline, drying salt film above it.
4.  Tropical Palm Trees (palmTree). 8–20 m. Slender gray-tan trunk curved by phototropism, leaf-scar rings every 10–20 cm, fibrous crown shaft. 14 arched pinnate fronds 3–5 m long: the rachis is a tapered beam (first bending mode about 0.8–1.5 Hz) with leaflets hanging from it at about 40° with a V-fold, flapping in a traveling wave from base to tip at 3–6 Hz. In gusts fronds streamline and leaflets fold together; a few dead fronds hang down; a fruit cluster under the crown. Strong glow when backlit.
5.  Charred Wildfire Snags (charredSnag). Blackened split trunks 6–25 m with broken bare branches. Surface charred into alligatored blocks (Worley cells with deep cracks), matte black with a faint blue-white sheen where cracks catch light; ember cracks glow on the blackbody ramp, pulsing 0.1–0.4 Hz with sparkle; ash drifts down; thin smoke wisps and heat shimmer. No sway, only a creak under strong wind. White ash at the base; steaming wet char in rain.
6.  Ferns (fern). Rosettes of 8–20 arching pinnate fronds 0.5–1.5 m, stiff rachis, 30–60 serrated pinnae per side tapering to the tip, paler undersides with spore dots in two rows; furred, tightly coiled fiddleheads in the center. Fronds nod with wind (0.8–2 Hz), pinnae flutter 5–10 Hz, raindrops knock whole fronds down and they rebound. Translucent glow when backlit.
7.  Monstera & Elephant-Ear Taro (broadleaf). Monstera: heart-shaped leaves 0.3–0.8 m, deep fenestrations (edge-to-midrib splits and oval holes from an SDF), glossy dark green, thick petioles with a kink at the leaf joint. Taro: peltate leaves (stalk joins inside the blade) 0.4–1 m, velvety blue-green with raised veins, superhydrophobic (rolling beads, drip tip). Large leaves sway slowly (1–2 Hz), dip under raindrop hits, flutter at the edges in gusts and shed water in sheets after rain.
8.  Wetland Reeds & Cattails (reeds). Blades 1–3 m long and 1–2 cm wide with a flexible midrib, bending as cantilever beams (first mode 0.5–1.2 Hz) and moving in coherent waves with the gusts; tips dry and brown. Cattail spikes: dark brown, velvety, cigar-shaped, 10–20 cm, on stiff stalks that barely sway; occasional white seed fluff drifting off. Waterline stained dark; blades cast reflections and thin shadows.
9.  Water Lilies & Pink Lotus (lilyPad). Pads 0.2–0.5 m, nearly circular with one radial notch, bright green top and purple-red underside showing where edges fold; they ride the surface, heaving and tilting with ripples and rain, with beads rolling on the waxy top. Lotus blossoms 15–25 cm: three layers of 15–20 translucent petals (white at the base to pink at the tip, SSS glow) around yellow stamens and a seed pod; they open slowly with morning light and close at dusk; stems 0.5–1.2 m with fine prickles.
10.  Scarlet Bromeliads & Purple Orchids (bromeliad). Bromeliad: rosette of 12–25 stiff, strap-shaped, glossy leaves forming a central tank that holds water (rain fills it, the level rises and spills), scarlet bracts and tiny violet flowers on a central spike, small marginal spines. Orchids: 3–7 arching sprays of 2–4 cm purple flowers with a ruffled lip, thick silver-green aerial roots clinging to bark, strap leaves. On forest floors, logs and trunks; they barely sway and the flowers tremble in gusts.
11.  Mossy Nurse Logs, Mushrooms & Cypress Knees (nurseLog). Fallen trunks 3–15 m in advanced decay: sagging, split, partly hollow, covered in moss mats (1–5 cm, bright to dark green with translucent tips), seedlings and ferns along the top, tiers of bracket fungi, mushroom clusters (caps 2–6 cm, gills, thin translucent flesh, slightly glossy when wet). Moss swells and brightens when wet and dulls when dry; wood goes dark after rain. Cypress knees as in entry 2.
12.  Spider Silk Webs (spiderWeb). Orb webs 20–60 cm with 20–30 radial threads and a capture spiral, slightly sagging. Threads are 2–6 µm, so draw sub-pixel, coverage-correct anti-aliased lines: invisible except when backlit, dewy or crossing dark backgrounds. Dew beads at regular spacing along the spiral, each a tiny lens; the web vibrates with wind (1–4 Hz) and rain hits and bends under bead weight; optional tiny spider at the hub. Strongest in morning mist.
A8. Fauna bible (26 cohorts)
Conventions. Lateral-sequence walk order is left hind, left fore, right hind, right fore (LH, LF, RH, RF) at phase offsets 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75; trots pair diagonal limbs half a cycle apart. Duty factor is the fraction of the cycle a foot is on the ground. Counts are per world. Flight distance is how close the player can get before the animal reacts. Every entry inherits everything in A5.
MAMMALS
Jaguars (jaguars, 6). Shoulder 0.65–0.75 m, head-body 1.2–1.7 m, short tail 0.45–0.7 m; massive head and jaws, stocky limbs, big pads, retractable claws. Coat golden-ochre to tawny, cream belly and inner legs, large black rosettes with one to three dots inside (left and right sides differ), solid spots on head and limbs, ringed tail; short dense glossy fur. Round pupils, amber iris, green-gold eyeshine. Walk 1 Hz stride, duty 0.65; trot 1.8–2.5 Hz; rare gallop bursts near 3 Hz. Stalk: belly 15–20 cm off the ground, shoulder blades rising above the spine line, head low, freeze-and-creep. Rounded ears rotate independently (dark backs with a pale spot); tail tip curls and flicks; slow blinks; breathing 20–30 per min at rest. Lap-drinks (about 4 laps/s), rests on a limb with legs dangling, swims. Forgotten Forest and river edge, dawn and dusk. Flight distance 25 m.
Florida Panthers (panthers, 5). Shoulder 0.6–0.7 m, head-body 1.2–1.5 m, long thick tail 0.6–0.8 m with a dark tip; small head, long hind legs. Uniform tawny coat, cream chin, chest and belly, dark ear backs, nose bridge and tail tip; one juvenile with faint spots and tail rings. Round pupils, pale gold-green eyes. Lateral-sequence walk 1 Hz, duty 0.6; fluid low trot; leaps and pounces rare. Ground-hugging stalk with the tail swinging low. Crosses shallow water high-stepping and shaking each paw. Cypress Swamp borders and hummocks. Very shy: flight distance 40 m.
White-Tailed Deer (deer, 12). Shoulder 0.8–1.0 m, slender legs. 1 buck with 8-point antlers in velvet (dark brown fuzz, visible vessels; main beams with tines, 30–50 cm), 7 does, 4 spotted fawns (0.45 m). Reddish-brown summer coat, white throat patch, eye ring, muzzle, belly and rump; 20 cm tail brown above and white below. Horizontal rectangular pupils, long lashes; ears 15–20 cm swivel independently ±90°. Walk 1.2 Hz, trot 2.2 Hz, flight by stotting (arched back, stiff-legged bounds up to 2 m high) with the tail flagged up and white hair fluffed. Graze cycle: head down 5–15 s, head up 1–3 s to scan with an ear sweep; rumination jaw circles about 1 Hz; tail flicks at flies; nose licks; breath 20–30 per min. Wildlife Clearing at dawn and dusk; snort and foot stomp on alarm. Flight distance 50 m.
Capybaras (capybaras, 16). Largest rodent: head-body 1.0–1.3 m, shoulder 0.5–0.6 m, barrel body, blunt boxy head, eyes, ears and nostrils high on the skull, tiny ears, no visible tail, partly webbed toes; adult males with a gland bump on the snout. Coarse sparse red-brown to gray-brown hair 3–12 cm with skin showing at the flanks, paler belly; flat dark spiky tufts when wet. Round dark eyes. Rolling walk 1.3 Hz; short gallops. Swims with only snout, eyes and ears above water, leaving a small V-wake; sits dog-like with straight forelegs. Side-to-side chewing, nostril flare, ear flicks, yawns, resting in heaps. Herds of 6–10 plus 4 young (0.3 m) grazing river banks, midday soak. Flight distance 20 m (they plunge into the water).
Black Bears (bears, 4). Shoulder 0.8–0.95 m, head-body 1.4–1.8 m, plantigrade with 5 toes and 4–6 cm curved claws, no shoulder hump, straight facial profile, tall rounded ears, small eyes. Glossy black coat (one cinnamon-brown), tan muzzle, sometimes a white chest blaze; skin ripples visibly over shoulders and rump in motion. Pigeon-toed rolling walk with a pendulum sway, 1 Hz, duty 0.65; trot; gallop bursts; stands on hind legs to survey for 2–6 s. Head-low sniffing with nostril flare and head swing, lip pursing, licking, tearing at nurse logs, scratching on trunks; breath 10–20 per min. Forest edge of the Wildlife Clearing; drinks at the pond; climbs trees with a hugging hitch. Flight distance 60 m.
Tapirs (tapirs, 5). Head-body 1.8–2.2 m, shoulder 0.9–1.1 m, barrel body arched at the rump, thick short legs (4 front toes, 3 hind), stub tail; a short prehensile proboscis (12–20 cm) that curls, probes and twitches constantly; small eyes set far back, rounded ears with white rims; short erect mane along the neck. Sparse bristly dark brown-gray hide with a slight sheen and paler cheeks; 1 calf is reddish-brown with cream stripes and spots. Walk 1 Hz with head low and the proboscis sweeping; trot; short gallops; wades and swims with the proboscis up as a snorkel and walks along the bed. Browses by grasping foliage, mud wallows. Amazon Canopy ridge. Flight distance 35 m; startles into a crashing run.
Raccoons (raccoons, 10). Head-body 0.45–0.6 m, ringed tail 0.2–0.3 m, hunched plantigrade, pointed muzzle, five long, nearly hand-like fingers. Grizzled gray-brown coat, black bandit mask edged in white, white muzzle, 5–7 black tail rings, dense underfur with frosted guard hairs; dark eyes with greenish-yellow eyeshine; rounded pale-rimmed ears. Waddling walk 1.8–2.2 Hz with a flexed back; bounding gallop; climbs head first and descends with hind feet rotated 180°. Dousing food in shallow water with delicate finger motion (0.5–2 Hz), whisker twitching, standing on hind legs, face washing, quick ear turns, chittering. Mangrove Passage at dusk. Flight distance 12 m.
River Otters (otters, 10). Head-body 0.6–0.9 m, tail 0.35–0.5 m, long fusiform body, short legs, webbed feet, small ears and nostrils that close underwater, broad flat head with long stiff whiskers. Dark chocolate-brown above, silvery cream throat and chest; dense two-layer fur that shows a silver sheen of trapped air underwater and clumps into wet spikes when out. Swim by dorsoventral body undulation with hind-foot paddling and tail steering at 2–4 body lengths/s, surfacing every 20–60 s with a blown breath; periscope bobs to look around. On land a loping bound (2–3 Hz) with an arched back, belly-slides down mud banks, rolls on grass to dry, then shakes. Floats on its back to eat, grooms, plays chases. Groups of 3–5 on the River Sanctuary shoreline. Flight distance 15 m.
Capuchin Monkeys (monkeys, 16). White-faced capuchin: head-body 0.35–0.5 m, semi-prehensile tail 0.4–0.55 m that coils at the tip, opposable thumbs, precision grip. Black body with cream-white face, throat, chest and shoulders, pink skin around eyes and nose, dark cap with a small peak, expressive brows. Quadrupedal bounding along branches (stride 2.5–3.5 Hz), leaps of 2–4 m with arms forward and branch recoil on landing, hand-over-hand climbing, brief tail-hangs. Expressions (brow raise, lip smack, teeth grin), examining objects with the fingers, mutual grooming, head tilts, hand-to-mouth feeding. Troops of 12–16 foraging fruit and insects through the Amazon Canopy, alarm calls, juveniles playing. Flight distance 25 m.
Three-Toed Sloths (sloths, 10). Brown-throated sloth: head-body 0.5–0.6 m, stub tail, rounded head, tiny ears, three 7–9 cm hooked claws on each limb, long forelimbs. Long coarse shaggy gray-brown outer hair with grooved shafts hosting green algae (green blotches, stronger in rain). Fur direction is reversed relative to most mammals: it parts along the belly midline and flows toward the back while hanging, so rain runs off. Dark stripe through each eye, pale face, upturned mouth. Ultra-slow: about 0.03–0.07 m/s at best, each limb reach taking 3–8 s with a hook-and-pull; long motionless hangs; head turns up to about 270°; slow blinks, yawns, a lazy claw scratch; curls into a branch fork to sleep (15–20 h). Amazon Canopy. Flight distance 5 m (barely reacts).
Manatees (manatees, 5). Length 2.8–3.5 m, spindle-shaped, a horizontal paddle tail about 0.6 m wide, two flippers with 3–4 nail tips, no hind limbs, blunt whiskered snout with a split prehensile upper lip, small eyes with a nictitating membrane, nostrils on top that seal with valves. Gray to gray-brown wrinkled skin, folds at neck and flippers, sparse fine hairs and stiff vibrissae, propeller scars, algae and barnacle patina in patches; glossy wet, matte when dry. Cruise with vertical tail strokes at 0.3–0.6 Hz (up to 2 Hz when startled), steer and scull with the flippers; rest near the bed and surface every 2–5 min (nostrils crest, a soft exhale, bubbles). Grazes submerged plants with its lip grasp, flipper-walks along the bottom, rolls to scratch. River Lagoon: 2–3 adults plus a calf. Flight distance 10 m.
BIRDS
Scarlet Macaws (macaws, 22). Length 80–90 cm, wingspan about 1 m, tail 40–50 cm long and tapered; large ivory upper bill and black lower bill, bare white face patch with fine lines of tiny red feathers, pale eye. Scarlet body, yellow median coverts, blue greater coverts and flight-feather tips, blue rump and tail tips; layered feathers with barb-direction anisotropy, angle-dependent structural blue and glowing pigment red. Flight: steady, powerful, shallow beats at about 3–5 Hz with brief glides, pairs flying wingtip to wingtip with synchronized beats, strings of 22 with loud squawks, 40–60 km/h, tail streaming, primaries separating on the upstroke. Perched: climbs using the bill as a third foot, grips food in one foot, preens, hangs upside down, stretches a wing, bobs its head. Over the Amazon Canopy at dawn and dusk. Flight distance 30 m.
Toco Toucans (toucans, 12). Length 55–65 cm; bill 17–20 cm, orange with a black tip, black base and yellow blush, ridge highlights, thin keratin that glows faintly when backlit. Black body, white throat and chest, red-orange undertail, blue skin ring around the eye with an orange outer ring, short rounded wings, long squared tail. Weak flier: bursts of 4–6 fast flaps at about 6 Hz then a glide, giving an undulating path; hops between branches with the tail cocked; tosses fruit back with a head flick; sleeps with the bill tucked under the wing and the tail flipped over the back. Amazon Canopy, pairs and small groups. Flight distance 20 m.
Bald Eagles (eagles, 8). Length 0.7–0.95 m, wingspan 1.8–2.3 m. 6 adults with white head and tail, dark chocolate body and wings, yellow hooked bill, eyes and feet; 2 immatures mottled brown and white. Soaring on thermals with wings flat, spiral banking at 15–30°, each of the 7 primary fingers splaying and flexing separately in turbulence, tail fanning for steering, glide ratio about 10:1, head swiveling to scan; slow deep flaps (about 2–3 Hz) only when needed. Stoops with wings half-folded and grabs fish with talons forward, trailing a splash; perches on dead snags near the fire edge, mantles, preens. Orbits high over the whole wilderness (80–250 m); the shadow glides across the canopy. Flight distance 50 m.
Spotted Owls (owls, 8). Length 40–48 cm, wingspan about 1.1 m; round facial disc, no ear tufts, large dark brown eyes, pale bill. Dark chocolate-brown plumage with white spots on head and back and white bars on the breast; feathered legs; soft fringes on the primaries for near-silent flight with slow wingbeats and long glides. Roosts upright on low limbs by day with eyes half-closed; head rotates up to about 270° with sudden counter-snaps; head bobs to triangulate sounds; slow blinks; fluffs up, coughs a pellet; swoops with wings and talons mantling over prey. Forgotten Forest hollows and cave alcoves, active at dusk. Flight distance 12 m.
Kingfishers (kingfishers, 10). Belted and ringed types: 28–40 cm, oversized head with a shaggy crest, long heavy bill, short legs and tail. Slate-blue upperparts, white collar, blue breast band, white belly (ringed type with a rufous belly). Perches on snags over the lagoon, head-bobbing to track fish; hovers 3–8 m up on rapid wingbeats (about 10 Hz); plunges head first with the nictitating membrane closing over the eyes; emerges in 1–2 s with a fish, flies to a perch and beats it. Cruising flight is low and direct with a rattling call, stiff fast beats (6–8 Hz) in undulating swoops, skimming the River Lagoon. Ring ripples on entry. Flight distance 20 m.
Flamingos (flamingos, 14). Height 1.1–1.5 m; long S-curved neck, long pink legs with a backward-bending joint that looks like a knee but is the ankle, webbed feet, down-kinked bill (black tip, pale base). Pink to coral-orange plumage with black flight feathers showing in flight and as a dark line along the folded wing. Wade slowly, head inverted, bill sweeping sideways while the feet stamp to stir sediment; group marching and synchronized head-flagging; rest on one leg with the neck folded back; flight with neck and legs extended, wingbeats 3–4 Hz, a running takeoff with splashing feet, lines and Vs. Wildlife Clearing pond with reflections. Flight distance 40 m.
Herons & Egrets (herons, 14). Great blue heron (1.0–1.2 m, wingspan 1.8 m), great egret (0.9–1.0 m, white, yellow bill, black legs), snowy egret (0.6 m, black bill, yellow feet). Nuptial aigrettes: long lacy back plumes streaming in the breeze; S-curved neck. Stalking: each foot lifted, hovering and lowered over 1–2 s while the body and head stay locked; the strike uncoils the neck in 50–100 ms; spear, juggle, swallow. Flight with the neck retracted in an S, legs trailing, slow deep wingbeats about 2 Hz; roosts in cypress with the neck tucked; hoarse croaks. Cypress Swamp and Mangrove Passage. Flight distance 25 m.